          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been tired and light-headed for months, my heart races on stairs, and my periods are heavy. Is that urgent, what could iron deficiency do to the blood, and which ICD-10 chapter covers anemia?",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, with a caveat.** The symptom check found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. It only covers a fixed list of presentations, and it advises seeing a medical professional if symptoms are severe, worsening, or new. Months of light-headedness and a racing heart on stairs are worth raising with a clinician soon. Book a visit within days, not months. The material I reviewed also says heavy periods alone are a question for a clinician to assess, not something it can grade.\n\n**Seek emergency care now** if you faint or nearly faint, have chest pressure or tightness, pain spreading to your arm, jaw, or back, breathlessness at rest, or a cold sweat with nausea. Light-headedness alongside chest discomfort can be a sign of a heart attack. Also get urgent care for black or tarry stools, or for soaking through pads or passing large clots faster than you can keep up. Tell a clinician if your breathlessness is getting worse, your ankles swell, or you gain weight over a few days.\n\n**What iron deficiency does to the blood:** iron is needed to make hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. When iron runs low, red cells become smaller and paler, which shows on a blood count. With less hemoglobin, less oxygen reaches your tissues, which can contribute to tiredness, light-headedness, and a heart that beats faster on exertion. Iron is mostly lost through bleeding, so a clinician will look for the source, and heavy menstrual bleeding is one possibility. Other sources, including slow bleeding in the digestive tract, also need checking. A blood count and iron tests can clarify this. Please don't start iron tablets on your own before that check.\n\n**ICD-10 chapter:** anemia is in Chapter 3, \"Diseases of the blood and blood-forming organs and certain disorders involving the immune mechanism,\" codes D50 to D89. Iron-deficiency anemia is D50. Two coding rules can move an anemia code elsewhere: anemia during pregnancy, childbirth, or the postpartum period is coded in the obstetric chapter, and a low hemoglobin with no anemia diagnosed is an abnormal finding coded in Chapter 18 until a diagnosis is documented.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
